PRACTICALITY NOTES: RULES THAT ARE HARD OR LOW-ROI
================================================================

Rules that are currently not very practical (or are difficult to implement reliably)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Rule 28 (Nonverbal behavior codes): Low practicality with text-only Descript input.
  Accurate `{PN:...}` / `{AvN:...}` extraction generally requires video-level annotation.

- Rule 17 (Full maze detection beyond simple repetitions): Difficult from ASR text alone.
  Many false starts and repairs are not consistently preserved in the source transcripts.

- Rule 21 (Unintelligible speech: `X/XX/XXX`): Usually not recoverable from clean ASR output.
  There is limited evidence in current transcripts to infer these markers confidently.

- Rule 22 (Omissions / partial words: `*`, `/*`): High linguistic ambiguity.
  Requires inferring what should have been said, which is error-prone without human coding.

- Rule 24 (Sound effects `%...`): Often low frequency and weakly represented in source text.
  Practical impact is limited unless the corpus explicitly contains these events.

- Rule 14 (Morphological marking): Not difficult technically, but low practical return here.
  Optional spaCy mode is now implemented and tested, but A/B results currently show lower
  overall similarity than morphology-off on this dataset.
